From 2d43e0a027e3d4443a353c6928b88a59d317bae6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 08:52:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix budget matching: hard price ceiling for low tiers, pure
 scoring for high tiers

Bug report: selecting the 200-500 budget still returned an 850 EUR Experience. Root cause -- the previous change removed the hard budget filter entirely in favor of pure scoring, so an Experience could still win as featured if other signals (mood/category/luxury priority) outweighed the budget penalty, regardless of price.

Fix is the hybrid the spec asked for, not a blanket revert:
- 200-500 and 500-1000: reinstated a hard price ceiling on the single Experience (tier.max exactly for 200-500, tier.max + 30% of the tier width for 500-1000, giving room for a small Enhancement) -- these budgets are still mostly "one Experience", so a genuinely out-of-range price must exclude it, not just get outscored.
- 1000-3000 and 3000+: unchanged, no ceiling -- these stay "overall proposal budget", scoring only, exactly as before (already correct per the tests).
- Both the ceiling and the scoring bonus/penalty now go through calculateProposalTotal (real pricing engine: per_person multiplies by guests+discounted children, tiers, seasonal overrides) instead of raw base_price, per the explicit per-person example in the follow-up message -- reused via a new getEstimatedExperiencePrice() wrapper, no pricing logic duplicated.
- No new field on Experience: the ceiling is computed from BUDGET_TIERS (already the single source of truth) against the real price, at matching time.

Verified with an isolated test replicating the real calculatePrice() formula: all 6 required tests pass (850/200-500 excluded, 400/200-500 / 850/500-1000 / 850,400/1000-3000 / 400/3000+ all compatible), plus edge cases (500 included, 501 excluded) and the per-person scenario (150/person: 300 for 2 adults passes 200-500, 600 for 4 adults fails it -- same Experience, different outcome by group size).
